{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":72914,"num_turns":2,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"dbcdaafd-7f2b-570f-891f-451ac2f7a2b8","total_cost_usd":0.5601630000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9539,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":7306,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9539,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7306,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9539,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9539},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":3963,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.004063000000000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":7306,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":9539,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.5561,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\":\"actor:dependence-free-batch-h1-reviewer-fable-36\",\"reviews\":[{\"case_id\":\"case:d8e451762e6f79802f9f\",\"verdict\":\"demonstrated_issue\",\"issue_class\":\"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\",\"bounded_statement\":\"Within the supplied files of case:d8e451762e6f79802f9f, the selected result (exact two-sided binomial test, 15/20 uv_bright first visits, p = 0.041389, declared significant at the 5% level) is produced by entering all 20 release rows as independent Bernoulli observations even though the declared independent unit is forager_id and the 20 rows come from only 6 foragers with 2–4 rows each. The row-independent binomial procedure therefore receives repeated entries per independent unit, and the significance claim does not hold under a unit-respecting reanalysis of the same data (per-forager majority sign test: 4 of 5 non-tied foragers favor uv_bright, two-sided p = 0.375).\",\"root_cause\":\"workflow/analysis.py counts every CSV row as one Bernoulli trial (total = len(trials); bright = row-level count) and submits the pooled row-level counts to scipy.stats.binomtest(bright, total, 0.5), a procedure whose validity assumes each of the n entries is an independent draw. Because the data-description designates forager_id as the independent unit and each forager contributes 2–4 rows, repeated non-independent observations from the same unit are entered as if independent, inflating the effective sample size from 6 units to 20 rows and deflating the reported p-value on which the significance conclusion rests.\",\"evidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The data description names forager_id as the independent-unit column and states that one row is a single release, so multiple rows per forager are repeated measures of one independent unit.\",\"observed_value\":\"Independent unit column: forager_id; one row = one release\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":12,\"end_line\":14,\"quoted_text\":\"One row is: one Y-maze release in which a single marked forager made a first landing on either the UV-bright or the UV-dull artificial corolla\\nIndependent unit column: forager_id\\nOne trial is: one row\"}]},{\"description\":\"The data description states that the 20 releases come from only 6 bees with between two and four releases per bee, confirming repeated entries per independent unit.\",\"observed_value\":\"20 releases from 6 bees, 2–4 releases per bee\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":8,\"end_line\":10,\"quoted_text\":\"Foragers were run in up to four\\ntest blocks, so most bees appear in the file several times; the number of\\nreleases per bee differs because some individuals stopped foraging early.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The raw data show the same forager_id appearing on multiple rows; for example FOR-01 contributes four rows (T001, T007, T013, T018).\",\"observed_value\":\"FOR-01 appears in 4 rows; 6 distinct forager_id values across 20 data rows\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":2,\"end_line\":2,\"quoted_text\":\"T001,FOR-01,B1,L,uv_bright,4.2\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":8,\"end_line\":8,\"quoted_text\":\"T007,FOR-01,B2,R,uv_bright,3.6\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":14,\"end_line\":14,\"quoted_text\":\"T013,FOR-01,B3,L,uv_bright,3.1\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":19,\"end_line\":19,\"quoted_text\":\"T018,FOR-01,B4,R,uv_bright,2.9\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow computes the binomial test on pooled row-level counts: n is the number of CSV rows and the success count is the number of rows labelled uv_bright, with no aggregation to the forager level and no dependence adjustment.\",\"observed_value\":\"binomtest(bright=15, total=20, 0.5) over row-level counts\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":82,\"end_line\":86,\"quoted_text\":\"    trials = read_trials(INPUT_PATH)\\n    total = len(trials)\\n    bright = sum(1 for row in trials if row[\\\"first_visit_choice\\\"] == BRIGHT_LABEL)\\n    tally = tally_by_forager(trials)\\n    outcome = binomtest(bright, total, CHANCE, alternative=\\\"two-sided\\\")\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report's selected result rests on the pooled row-level binomial test and claims significance at the 5% level with p = 0.041389.\",\"observed_value\":\"p = 0.041389 from 15/20 pooled rows; claimed significant\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":29,\"end_line\":29,\"quoted_text\":\"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial test of 15 uv_bright first visits out of 20 trials against p = 0.5: p = 0.041389 (observed share 0.750); the deviation from chance is significant at the 5% level.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report's Analysis section states explicitly that every trial row was entered as one Bernoulli observation, i.e., each row was treated as an independent draw despite repeated rows per forager.\",\"observed_value\":\"row-as-Bernoulli-observation entry into the binomial test\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":20,\"end_line\":22,\"quoted_text\":\"Every trial row was entered as one Bernoulli observation and the pooled counts\\nwere submitted to an exact two-sided binomial test (`scipy.stats.binomtest`)\\nagainst the chance expectation p = 0.5.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report's own per-forager table shows the unit-level structure (FOR-01: 4/4, FOR-02: 3/4, FOR-03: 4/4, FOR-04: 2/3, FOR-05: 1/3, FOR-06: 1/2), from which a unit-respecting sign test on per-forager majorities (4 of 5 non-tied foragers favoring uv_bright, FOR-06 tied) yields two-sided p = 0.375, not significant — showing the pooled row-level test materially drives the significance claim.\",\"observed_value\":\"unit-level reanalysis: sign test p = 0.375 vs reported pooled p = 0.041389\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":9,\"end_line\":16,\"quoted_text\":\"| forager_id | trials | uv_bright first visits |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FOR-01 | 4 | 4 |\\n| FOR-02 | 4 | 3 |\\n| FOR-03 | 4 | 4 |\\n| FOR-04 | 3 | 2 |\\n| FOR-05 | 3 | 1 |\\n| FOR-06 | 2 | 1 |\"}]}],\"counterevidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The report openly discloses the repeated-measures structure in its Notes section, acknowledging that several rows describe the same insect and that the test weighs each row as one independent draw. This is transparency about the limitation, but it is not a mitigation: no unit-level aggregation, mixed model, or other dependence-aware procedure was applied, and the significance claim in the selected result is stated unconditionally.\",\"observed_value\":\"acknowledged but uncorrected dependence\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":33,\"end_line\":35,\"quoted_text\":\"The 20 trials come from 6 foragers, so several rows describe the same insect\\ntested again; the test above nonetheless weighs each row as one independent\\ndraw. Trial order within a forager was not modelled.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow does compute a per-forager tally (tally_by_forager), which could suggest awareness of the unit structure; however, the tally is used only for the descriptive table in the report and plays no role in the statistical test, which uses the pooled row counts.\",\"observed_value\":\"per-forager tally used descriptively only, not in the test\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":25,\"end_line\":33,\"quoted_text\":\"def tally_by_forager(trials):\\n    tally = {}\\n    for row in trials:\\n        forager = row[\\\"forager_id\\\"]\\n        counts = tally.setdefault(forager, {\\\"trials\\\": 0, \\\"bright\\\": 0})\\n        counts[\\\"trials\\\"] += 1\\n        if row[\\\"first_visit_choice\\\"] == BRIGHT_LABEL:\\n            counts[\\\"bright\\\"] += 1\\n    return tally\"}]}],\"falsification_attempt\":\"I attempted to falsify the finding in three ways. (1) Could each row legitimately be an independent unit? No: the data description explicitly names forager_id as the independent-unit column and states that most bees appear several times; the CSV confirms 6 distinct forager_id values across 20 rows (e.g., FOR-01 on rows for T001, T007, T013, T018). (2) Does the workflow anywhere adjust for or aggregate over foragers before testing? No: main() sets total = len(trials) and bright to the row-level uv_bright count and passes these directly to binomtest; the per-forager tally is used only for a descriptive table. (3) Is the issue immaterial, i.e., would the conclusion survive a unit-respecting analysis? No: recomputing from the report's own per-forager table, 4 of 5 non-tied foragers show a uv_bright majority (FOR-06 is tied at 1/2), giving an exact two-sided sign test p = 2 × (C(5,4)+C(5,5))/2^5 = 0.375, and the per-forager mean of within-forager uv_bright shares (0.736) rests on only 6 units; the reported 5%-level significance therefore depends on treating the 20 dependent rows as independent. I also verified the reported counts (15/20; per-forager 4/4, 3/4, 4/4, 2/3, 1/3, 1/2 sums to 15/20) and that binomtest(15, 20, 0.5) yields p ≈ 0.0414 as reported, so the issue is in the design of the test entry, not an arithmetic error. All falsification attempts failed, so the issue stands.\",\"cross_case_evidence_used\":false,\"unresolved_material_questions\":[],\"self_reported_confidence\":\"high\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-h1-reviewer-fable-36","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:d8e451762e6f79802f9f","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"Within the supplied files of case:d8e451762e6f79802f9f, the selected result (exact two-sided binomial test, 15/20 uv_bright first visits, p = 0.041389, declared significant at the 5% level) is produced by entering all 20 release rows as independent Bernoulli observations even though the declared independent unit is forager_id and the 20 rows come from only 6 foragers with 2–4 rows each. The row-independent binomial procedure therefore receives repeated entries per independent unit, and the significance claim does not hold under a unit-respecting reanalysis of the same data (per-forager majority sign test: 4 of 5 non-tied foragers favor uv_bright, two-sided p = 0.375).","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py counts every CSV row as one Bernoulli trial (total = len(trials); bright = row-level count) and submits the pooled row-level counts to scipy.stats.binomtest(bright, total, 0.5), a procedure whose validity assumes each of the n entries is an independent draw. Because the data-description designates forager_id as the independent unit and each forager contributes 2–4 rows, repeated non-independent observations from the same unit are entered as if independent, inflating the effective sample size from 6 units to 20 rows and deflating the reported p-value on which the significance conclusion rests.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description names forager_id as the independent-unit column and states that one row is a single release, so multiple rows per forager are repeated measures of one independent unit.","observed_value":"Independent unit column: forager_id; one row = one release","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":12,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"One row is: one Y-maze release in which a single marked forager made a first landing on either the UV-bright or the UV-dull artificial corolla\nIndependent unit column: forager_id\nOne trial is: one row"}]},{"description":"The data description states that the 20 releases come from only 6 bees with between two and four releases per bee, confirming repeated entries per independent unit.","observed_value":"20 releases from 6 bees, 2–4 releases per bee","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":8,"end_line":10,"quoted_text":"Foragers were run in up to four\ntest blocks, so most bees appear in the file several times; the number of\nreleases per bee differs because some individuals stopped foraging early."}]},{"description":"The raw data show the same forager_id appearing on multiple rows; for example FOR-01 contributes four rows (T001, T007, T013, T018).","observed_value":"FOR-01 appears in 4 rows; 6 distinct forager_id values across 20 data rows","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":2,"quoted_text":"T001,FOR-01,B1,L,uv_bright,4.2"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":8,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"T007,FOR-01,B2,R,uv_bright,3.6"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":14,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"T013,FOR-01,B3,L,uv_bright,3.1"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":19,"end_line":19,"quoted_text":"T018,FOR-01,B4,R,uv_bright,2.9"}]},{"description":"The workflow computes the binomial test on pooled row-level counts: n is the number of CSV rows and the success count is the number of rows labelled uv_bright, with no aggregation to the forager level and no dependence adjustment.","observed_value":"binomtest(bright=15, total=20, 0.5) over row-level counts","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":82,"end_line":86,"quoted_text":"    trials = read_trials(INPUT_PATH)\n    total = len(trials)\n    bright = sum(1 for row in trials if row[\"first_visit_choice\"] == BRIGHT_LABEL)\n    tally = tally_by_forager(trials)\n    outcome = binomtest(bright, total, CHANCE, alternative=\"two-sided\")"}]},{"description":"The report's selected result rests on the pooled row-level binomial test and claims significance at the 5% level with p = 0.041389.","observed_value":"p = 0.041389 from 15/20 pooled rows; claimed significant","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":29,"end_line":29,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial test of 15 uv_bright first visits out of 20 trials against p = 0.5: p = 0.041389 (observed share 0.750); the deviation from chance is significant at the 5% level."}]},{"description":"The report's Analysis section states explicitly that every trial row was entered as one Bernoulli observation, i.e., each row was treated as an independent draw despite repeated rows per forager.","observed_value":"row-as-Bernoulli-observation entry into the binomial test","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":20,"end_line":22,"quoted_text":"Every trial row was entered as one Bernoulli observation and the pooled counts\nwere submitted to an exact two-sided binomial test (`scipy.stats.binomtest`)\nagainst the chance expectation p = 0.5."}]},{"description":"The report's own per-forager table shows the unit-level structure (FOR-01: 4/4, FOR-02: 3/4, FOR-03: 4/4, FOR-04: 2/3, FOR-05: 1/3, FOR-06: 1/2), from which a unit-respecting sign test on per-forager majorities (4 of 5 non-tied foragers favoring uv_bright, FOR-06 tied) yields two-sided p = 0.375, not significant — showing the pooled row-level test materially drives the significance claim.","observed_value":"unit-level reanalysis: sign test p = 0.375 vs reported pooled p = 0.041389","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":9,"end_line":16,"quoted_text":"| forager_id | trials | uv_bright first visits |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| FOR-01 | 4 | 4 |\n| FOR-02 | 4 | 3 |\n| FOR-03 | 4 | 4 |\n| FOR-04 | 3 | 2 |\n| FOR-05 | 3 | 1 |\n| FOR-06 | 2 | 1 |"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report openly discloses the repeated-measures structure in its Notes section, acknowledging that several rows describe the same insect and that the test weighs each row as one independent draw. This is transparency about the limitation, but it is not a mitigation: no unit-level aggregation, mixed model, or other dependence-aware procedure was applied, and the significance claim in the selected result is stated unconditionally.","observed_value":"acknowledged but uncorrected dependence","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":33,"end_line":35,"quoted_text":"The 20 trials come from 6 foragers, so several rows describe the same insect\ntested again; the test above nonetheless weighs each row as one independent\ndraw. Trial order within a forager was not modelled."}]},{"description":"The workflow does compute a per-forager tally (tally_by_forager), which could suggest awareness of the unit structure; however, the tally is used only for the descriptive table in the report and plays no role in the statistical test, which uses the pooled row counts.","observed_value":"per-forager tally used descriptively only, not in the test","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":25,"end_line":33,"quoted_text":"def tally_by_forager(trials):\n    tally = {}\n    for row in trials:\n        forager = row[\"forager_id\"]\n        counts = tally.setdefault(forager, {\"trials\": 0, \"bright\": 0})\n        counts[\"trials\"] += 1\n        if row[\"first_visit_choice\"] == BRIGHT_LABEL:\n            counts[\"bright\"] += 1\n    return tally"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the finding in three ways. (1) Could each row legitimately be an independent unit? No: the data description explicitly names forager_id as the independent-unit column and states that most bees appear several times; the CSV confirms 6 distinct forager_id values across 20 rows (e.g., FOR-01 on rows for T001, T007, T013, T018). (2) Does the workflow anywhere adjust for or aggregate over foragers before testing? No: main() sets total = len(trials) and bright to the row-level uv_bright count and passes these directly to binomtest; the per-forager tally is used only for a descriptive table. (3) Is the issue immaterial, i.e., would the conclusion survive a unit-respecting analysis? No: recomputing from the report's own per-forager table, 4 of 5 non-tied foragers show a uv_bright majority (FOR-06 is tied at 1/2), giving an exact two-sided sign test p = 2 × (C(5,4)+C(5,5))/2^5 = 0.375, and the per-forager mean of within-forager uv_bright shares (0.736) rests on only 6 units; the reported 5%-level significance therefore depends on treating the 20 dependent rows as independent. I also verified the reported counts (15/20; per-forager 4/4, 3/4, 4/4, 2/3, 1/3, 1/2 sums to 15/20) and that binomtest(15, 20, 0.5) yields p ≈ 0.0414 as reported, so the issue is in the design of the test entry, not an arithmetic error. All falsification attempts failed, so the issue stands.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":29527,"ttft_stream_ms":1588,"time_to_request_ms":19,"type":"result","duration_ms":72991,"uuid":"1ef869f9-d1a3-47e8-b813-b029d08c3434"}
